const e={note:"Nothing in this hall is quite where it looks, and the reader should hold three gaps open at once. The Avestan alphabet was cut in the Sasanian period to fix a liturgy that was already something like a thousand years old, and the oldest surviving copy of anything written in it dates from the fourteenth century — so between the poet and the page stand a millennium of memory and then seven centuries of scribes, and each current has to say which of the three it is describing. The transliteration will look strange until it is understood as literal: uu and ii are not doubled letters but exactly what the manuscripts write, and t̰, ṣ̌ and xᵛ are single characters in the script rather than clusters. Where the Gāthās are quoted, assume that a translation is an argument — Insler, Humbach and Kellens produce texts that differ not in shading but in sense, and no rendering offered here is settled. The Pahlavi books that supply most of what people know as Zoroastrian doctrine — Bundahišn, Dēnkard, Ardā Wirāz Nāmag — are magnificent and they are ninth-century, written under Islamic rule; the currents mark where a doctrine is theirs rather than the Avesta's, because reading them back into the Gāthās is the commonest error in the whole subject. And this is not a dead religion. The Yasna is performed daily. The priests, the fires and the communities in Mumbai, Udvada, Yazd, Tehran and the diaspora are present tense, and the currents keep them so.",themes:[{slug:"the-invented-alphabet",title:"The Alphabet Made for a Voice",native:"𐬬𐬀𐬗𐬀𐬵",translit:"vacah",summary:"Fifty-three letters, sixteen of them vowels, cut in the Sasanian period to arrest a liturgy that had never been written — and whose oldest surviving page is seven centuries younger than the alphabet itself.",paragraphs:["The Avestan alphabet is the only script in this Scriptorium that was designed to a brief, for a body of text that already existed and was in daily use. Everything about it answers to that brief. It runs right to left, like the Book Pahlavi from which its letter-shapes are drawn, but its letters do not join: each stands separate, with a dot between words, because a joined script generates ambiguities and the point of this one was to have none. It carries no heterograms — Book Pahlavi wrote the Aramaic word for king and expected the reader to say šāh, which is a convenience for a chancery and a catastrophe for a liturgy in which the sound is the thing. And where Book Pahlavi had contracted to something like fourteen distinct signs doing the work of thirty-odd sounds, one grapheme serving g and d and y, another serving n and w and r, the Avestan alphabet has fifty-three letters, sixteen of them vowels, and every one of them phonetic. It is a phonetic transcription in the modern technical sense, produced roughly fourteen hundred years before anyone set out to invent one.","When it was designed is disputed, though not widely. Karl Hoffmann placed the invention in the fourth century, under Shāpūr II, and connected it with the ecclesiastical activity the Pahlavi books credit to the priest Ādurbād ī Mahraspandān; Prods Oktor Skjærvø and others have argued for the sixth, under Khosrow I, which is where most current handbooks leave it. Nobody argues for anything outside the Sasanian period. Behind that question stands a larger one. Hoffmann and Johanna Narten, in Der sasanidische Archetypus (1989), argued that every surviving Avestan manuscript descends from one written copy made when the alphabet was new, and that the orthographic peculiarities shared by all of them are the fingerprints of that single book. Alberto Cantera has since led the case against: on his reading the manuscripts descend from ritual practice rather than from a volume, transmitted along the lines of the ceremonies that used them, and the archetype may be a philological convenience rather than an object that ever existed. The argument is live, and it determines what an edition of the Avesta can honestly claim to be.","Sixteen vowel letters is the detail that gives the intention away. The alphabet writes short and long a, i and u; e and ē; o and ō; the schwa ə and a long ə̄; two nasalised a-vowels; and two more for an open rounded quality between a and o. Book Pahlavi had at most three vowel signs, used loosely. Among the consonants the script separates four sibilants where Pahlavi has one, distinguishes the voiced fricatives δ, β and γ from the stops behind them, and reserves a letter for the weakened final t that Iranists transliterate t̰ — a sound with no grammatical work whatever to do, audible only when the text is chanted. That letter is the clearest evidence of purpose the alphabet contains: somebody wanted the recording, not the wording. One letter, l, is not Avestan at all and was added later, for writing Middle Persian in Avestan characters — the practice called Pāzand, by which the priesthood escaped the ambiguity of its own everyday script. Neryosang Dhaval, working in Gujarat in the twelfth century, used it to transcribe the Pahlavi Yasna, and then rendered that into Sanskrit.","And then the shock. No manuscript of the Avesta survives from before the thirteenth century. The manuscripts J2 and K5, both copied at Cambay in 1323 by the priest Mihrābān Kayxusraw, give the Yasna with the Pahlavi version beneath the Avestan, and they are the foundation on which Karl Friedrich Geldner built the critical edition of 1886–96 that has still not been superseded. There is one exception, and it says nothing in the alphabet's favour: a fragment recovered from Dunhuang and now in the British Library carries the Ashem Vohu prayer, generally dated to the ninth century, written in Sogdian script and in a phonetic dress that appears to have passed through a Middle Persian intermediary; it was published by Ilya Gershevitch. The oldest surviving copy of any Avestan text is therefore not written in the Avestan alphabet, was made some two thousand miles east of Fārs, and is a short prayer that somebody carried. Cantera's Avestan Digital Archive has since photographed and catalogued the manuscript tradition on a scale Geldner could not attempt, which is the first substantial change in the field's evidence base in a hundred years.","That gap is this hall's subject. An alphabet built to arrest a text at a moment, and then seven hundred years of silence before the earliest page of it anyone can hold. What fills the gap is not writing but recitation, and the manuscripts themselves are the argument for how well it worked. They preserve Old Avestan grammatical forms that fourteenth-century copyists could not have reconstructed and demonstrably did not understand; they preserve metrical structures that only emerge when the orthography's own later contractions are undone; they preserve epenthetic vowels and a drawled long schwa that belong to chanting rather than to grammar, and that no scribe would have invented. The tradition's explanation for the missing centuries is that Alexander burned the archetype, written — the Dēnkard and the Ardā Wirāz Nāmag say — in gold on prepared ox-hides at Persepolis. Historians read this as a Sasanian-period rationalisation of the fact that there had been no book to burn; the tradition reads it as history. What is not disputed is the scale of the loss: Dēnkard 8 summarises twenty-one nasks, and of those only one, the Vīdēvdād, has come down entire.","In the current shape of the field, three positions can be held at once without contradiction, and most Iranists hold some version of all three: that the alphabet is a Sasanian instrument of unusual sophistication; that the text it records is very much older than the instrument; and that the written transmission we possess is short, thin and late enough that the burden of proof for accuracy falls on internal linguistic evidence rather than on any manuscript. Everything downstream in this hall — the antiquity of the Gāthās, the dating of Zarathustra, the relation between the Avesta and the Pahlavi books — is affected by which of the three is being leaned on at a given moment."],terms:[{native:"𐬓",translit:"xᵛ",gloss:"a single letter for the labialised velar fricative, the first sound of xᵛarənah; Pahlavi had to write it with two signs neither of which was unambiguous. Unicode: AVESTAN LETTER XVE."},{native:"𐬴",translit:"ṣ̌",gloss:"one of four sibilants the alphabet keeps apart; it writes the sound that developed from an older cluster *rt, as in aṣ̌a — a distinction with no purpose except exactness. Unicode: AVESTAN LETTER SSHE."},{native:"𐬝",translit:"t̰",gloss:"the weakened final t. It carries no grammatical information and exists only because a reciter pronounced it that way; the single most telling letter in the alphabet. Unicode: AVESTAN LETTER TTE."},{native:"𐬇",translit:"ə̄",gloss:"the long schwa: one of sixteen vowel letters, and a vowel whose length is largely a fact about chanting rather than about morphology. Unicode: AVESTAN LETTER AEE."},{native:"𐬜",translit:"δ",gloss:"the voiced dental fricative, kept distinct from d — a contrast Book Pahlavi cannot express, and one of many the new alphabet recovered. Unicode: AVESTAN LETTER DHE."},{native:"𐬮",translit:"l",gloss:"the letter that is not Avestan. Added for writing Middle Persian in Avestan characters (Pāzand), which is how the priesthood escaped the ambiguity of its own script. Unicode: AVESTAN LETTER LE."}],texts:[{title:"Karl Hoffmann and Johanna Narten, Der sasanidische Archetypus (Wiesbaden, 1989)",note:"The case that all surviving manuscripts descend from a single Sasanian written copy, argued from the orthography and phonetics of the alphabet itself. The book against which the subsequent debate defines itself."},{title:"Alberto Cantera (ed.), The Transmission of the Avesta (Wiesbaden, 2012)",note:"The volume in which the single-archetype hypothesis is contested and a liturgical, multi-stranded model of transmission is set out. Read alongside Hoffmann–Narten, not instead of it."},{title:"K. F. Geldner, Avesta: The Sacred Books of the Parsis, 3 vols (Stuttgart, 1886–1896)",note:"The critical edition, built on the manuscripts and never replaced. Its apparatus is where the actual state of the textual evidence can be inspected."},{title:"Manuscripts J2 and K5, copied at Cambay in 1323 by Mihrābān Kayxusraw",note:"Yasna with the Pahlavi version; among the oldest Avestan manuscripts in existence and the backbone of every edition since. That the tradition's earliest witnesses are fourteenth-century Indian copies is the central fact of the field."},{title:"The Sogdian Ashem Vohu fragment from Dunhuang (British Library)",note:"Ninth century, in Sogdian script rather than Avestan, published by Ilya Gershevitch. The oldest surviving copy of any Avestan text, and it is not written in the alphabet made for it."}]},{slug:"the-gathas",title:"The Old Language",native:"𐬔𐬁𐬚𐬁",translit:"gāθā",summary:"Seventeen hymns in a language centuries older than everything around them, in metres that can still be counted, and in a syntax on which no two translators agree.",paragraphs:["The Gāthās are seventeen hymns, set inside the Yasna at chapters 28–34, 43–46, 47–50, 51 and 53, and grouped into five collections named for their metres: Ahunauuaitī, Uštauuaitī, Spəṇtā.mainiiū, Vohuxšaθrā and Vahištōišti. Between the first group and the second sits the Yasna Haptaŋhāiti, the Worship in Seven Chapters, in the same archaic language but in prose. Together with four short prayers these texts make up the whole of what Iranists call Old Avestan — a few thousand words in all, against the far larger Younger Avestan corpus of the Yašts, the Vīdēvdād and the rest of the Yasna. The distance between the two languages is not stylistic. It is the distance between Homeric and Attic Greek and probably rather more, and it is visible in every grammatical category: Old Avestan keeps an inflectional apparatus that Younger Avestan has already lost, and Younger Avestan writers imitating the old language — as in the declaration at Yasna 12 — produce forms no Old Avestan speaker would have made.","How old the language is can be estimated, if not dated, by comparison. Old Avestan stands to the Sanskrit of the Rigveda roughly as one dialect of a shared ancestor stands to another: the correspondences are so regular that lines can be converted from one to the other by a small set of sound laws, and the shared poetic formulae — the priest as the one who yokes his thoughts like a chariot team, the word as the weapon — are not borrowings but a common inheritance. The vocabulary is the same vocabulary at a slight remove: haoma to soma, aṣ̌a to ṛta, mąθra to mantra, ahura to asura, daēuua to deva. This closeness is why the Gāthās matter far outside Iranian studies, and it is also the whole of the linguistic case for their antiquity, since a language cannot be carbon-dated and the comparison gives a relative position, not a century.","The metre is countable and this turns out to be evidentially important. The Ahunauuaitī hymns run in stanzas of three lines of sixteen syllables with a caesura after the seventh; the Uštauuaitī in five lines of eleven, caesura after the fourth; the Spəṇtā.mainiiū the same but in four lines; the Vohuxšaθrā in three lines of fourteen, caesura at the seventh; the last collection is metrically the most irregular and the analyses differ. Scansion depends on syllable count alone, not on quantity or stress. The point of consequence is that the metre only comes out right when certain later orthographic contractions in the manuscripts are undone — which means the transmitted spelling preserves, beneath its own conventions, a form of the text older than the conventions. The scribes were copying something they could not have generated, and could not fully read.","Then the difficulty. The Gāthās are among the hardest texts in any Indo-European language, and the translations do not disagree at the margins. Two approaches have contended for a century and a half. The traditional or exegetical school reads the hymns through the Pahlavi version and the indigenous commentary, on the argument that a living interpretive tradition, however late, is better evidence than a reconstruction; Ilya Gershevitch and H. W. Bailey are among its more formidable representatives. The comparative or Vedic school reads them against Rigvedic language and poetics, treating the Pahlavi translation as an early and often confused attempt at the same problem the modern reader faces; Christian Bartholomae, Stanley Insler and Helmut Humbach belong here. Yasna 30.3, the stanza on the two mainiius, is the standard demonstration: it is usually rendered as speaking of two spirits, twins, heard of at the beginning, better and bad in thought and word and deed — and the word usually taken as 'in a dream' or 'in sleep' has been construed half a dozen ways, with the rest of the stanza following it.","The largest challenge came from Paris. Jean Kellens and Eric Pirart, in Les textes vieil-avestiques (1988–1991), argued that the Gāthās are liturgical compositions rather than the utterances of a historical individual, that the first-person voice in them is a ritual role rather than a biography, and that the whole enterprise of reading them as a prophet's personal record was a nineteenth-century imposition. Mary Boyce, Insler and Almut Hintze, among others, have defended a historical poet-priest who names his patron and his family and speaks in his own person. The dispute is unresolved and the reader should treat it as such. What the Yasna Haptaŋhāiti quietly adds is a complication for both sides: composed in the same old language, placed at the very centre of the liturgy, communal in voice, addressed to fire and waters and the earth, and never once naming Zarathustra."],terms:[{native:"𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬥𐬌𐬌𐬎",translit:"mainiiu",gloss:"spirit, mentality, disposition; the Gāthic contrast is between two of these, not between a god and a devil, and the later Ohrmazd–Ahriman opposition is a simplification of it"},{native:"𐬨𐬀𐬔𐬀",translit:"maga",gloss:"a Gāthic word of genuinely undetermined meaning — 'gift', 'exchange', 'fellowship', 'the great work'; Bartholomae took it as the community of the faithful, later readers as the ritual reciprocity itself"},{native:"𐬨𐬄𐬚𐬭𐬀𐬥",translit:"mąθran",gloss:"the possessor of the mąθra: the poet-priest who composes and speaks the formulated word. The first-person speaker of the Gāthās claims the title, which is why the biographical question turns on it"},{native:"𐬎𐬱𐬙𐬁",translit:"uštā",gloss:"weal, the state of being well; the opening word of Yasna 43 and the name of the second Gāthā, in a line that wishes it to whoever wishes it to another"},{native:"𐬵𐬀𐬞𐬙𐬀𐬢𐬵𐬁𐬌𐬙𐬌",translit:"haptaŋhāiti",gloss:"'of seven chapters'; the Old Avestan prose liturgy at Yasna 35–41, placed at the centre of the ceremony and edited by Almut Hintze in 2007"},{native:"𐬀𐬵𐬎𐬥𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬍",translit:"ahunauuaitī",gloss:"the first Gāthā, named from the Ahuna Vairiia prayer that shares its metre; the five collections are named by metre rather than by subject, which is itself a fact about how they were classified"}],texts:[{title:"Yasna 28–34, 43–46, 47–50, 51, 53",note:"The seventeen hymns, transmitted only inside the Yasna and never as a separate book. Their position in the liturgy is part of the evidence about what they were for."},{title:"Stanley Insler, The Gāthās of Zarathustra (Acta Iranica 8, Leiden, 1975)",note:"The most influential English rendering of the twentieth century, with commentary; comparative in method and confident where others hedge."},{title:"Helmut Humbach, The Gāthās of Zarathushtra and the Other Old Avestan Texts, 2 vols (Heidelberg, 1991)",note:"Text, translation and a commentary volume that sets the philological options out rather than choosing silently among them. The place to see how much of a translation is decision."},{title:"Jean Kellens and Eric Pirart, Les textes vieil-avestiques, 3 vols (Wiesbaden, 1988–1991)",note:"The edition behind the liturgical reading, and the sharpest challenge to the prophetic-biographical understanding of the hymns."},{title:"Almut Hintze, A Zoroastrian Liturgy: The Worship in Seven Chapters (Wiesbaden, 2007)",note:"The critical edition of the Yasna Haptaŋhāiti — the Old Avestan text that is not by, and does not mention, Zarathustra."}]},{slug:"zarathustra",title:"The Argument About When",native:"𐬰𐬀𐬭𐬀𐬚𐬎𐬱𐬙𐬭𐬀",translit:"zaraθuštra",summary:"The tradition puts him 258 years before Alexander; the language of his hymns puts him nearer 1000 BCE or earlier. Nobody has closed the gap, and it is the widest respectable disagreement in the field.",paragraphs:["What the Gāthās say about their speaker is little and specific. He calls himself a zaotar, the priest who recites at the pressing, and a mąθran, one who commands the formulated word. He names his clan, Spitāma. He names his patron, Kauui Vīštāspa, his own lineage the Haēcat̰aspa Spitāmas, and Vīštāspa's kin the Huuōguua; he addresses his daughter, Pouručistā, in the last hymn, which concerns her marriage. He denounces by title, not by name, three classes of religious functionary — the kauuis, the karapans and the usijs — whose sacrifices he holds to be violent and false, and he complains, at Yasna 46.1, that he does not know to what land to turn, being driven out with few cattle and few men. This is not a biography. It is what a working priest lets slip while arguing, and every reconstruction of the man is built on it.","The tradition's date is exact and is transmitted with unusual consistency: 258 years before Alexander. It appears in the Bundahišn, and through Sasanian channels in al-Bīrūnī, al-Ṭabarī and al-Mas'ūdī. Taking Alexander's conquest as 330 BCE, the figure places the conversion of Vīštāspa at 588 and the birth around 628 BCE — a sixth-century prophet, contemporary with the Achaemenid rise. W. B. Henning defended the date's general plausibility in Zoroaster: Politician or Witch-Doctor? (1951), against speculative reconstructions he thought worse; Gherardo Gnoli, in Zoroaster in History (2000), argued for it at length. The counter-case, made by H. W. Bailey and others, is that 258 has the shape of a Seleucid-era calculation working backwards from a known era rather than a memory, and that its consistency across sources shows only that they copied one another.","The linguistic argument runs the other way and is the reason the dispute is unresolvable rather than merely unresolved. Old Avestan stands at roughly the same stage of Indo-Iranian development as the language of the Rigveda, and the Rigveda is generally placed in the second millennium BCE. Mary Boyce, in the first volume of A History of Zoroastrianism (1975), pushed the consequence hard and proposed something like 1700–1500 BCE; Prods Oktor Skjærvø and others prefer a looser 'late second millennium', on the ground that comparative archaism gives a position in a sequence rather than a date on a calendar. Almost nobody working from the language alone will come down as late as the sixth century. The honest statement of the field's position is therefore two statements: the tradition reckons 628 BCE, and the philological argument gives something near or before 1000 BCE, with a thousand years unaccounted for between them.","The negative evidence is worth setting out because it constrains both parties. No Achaemenid inscription names Zarathustra. Darius invokes Ahuramazdā continually, in language saturated with what look like Zoroastrian categories, and never mentions the prophet — which sits badly with a sixth-century figure at the Persian court and not particularly badly with a much older one. Greek notices are worse than useless as chronology and instructive as reception: the Alcibiades I attributed to Plato calls him son of Oromazes; Pliny reports a figure of six thousand years before Plato's death, Plutarch something similar, and Xanthus of Lydia is cited for six thousand years before Xerxes. Even the name argues quietly. Its second element is uštra, camel, as clearly as Sanskrit uṣṭra; the first is disputed, Bartholomae's 'possessing old camels' being the best-known guess. It is an ordinary, unheroic pastoral name, and Boyce among others took that as evidence that a real person bore it, since inventions are not named this way.","Where he lived is the one question on which the evidence points steadily in a single direction. The sixteen lands listed at the opening of the Vīdēvdād are all eastern — Sogdiana, Margiana, Bactria, Areia, Arachosia, the Helmand basin — and not one is in western Iran or Persia proper. The mountain and river geography of Yašt 10 belongs to the same world. Gnoli argued for Sīstān and the Helmand; Boyce for the steppe of Central Asia; several have proposed the region of the Bactria–Margiana archaeological complex, though no material find has ever been tied to the texts. And behind all of this sits Kellens's objection, which cannot simply be waved off: if the Gāthās are liturgical poetry rather than an individual's testimony, then there may be no person to date, and a century and a half of chronological argument has been conducted about a literary voice. Most Iranists do not accept that conclusion. None of them can refute it from the texts alone."],terms:[{native:"𐬯𐬞𐬌𐬙𐬁𐬨𐬀",translit:"spitāma",gloss:"the clan name the Gāthic speaker gives himself; still the surname of Zoroastrian families and the only genealogical fact the hymns supply"},{native:"𐬬𐬍𐬱𐬙𐬁𐬯𐬞𐬀",translit:"vīštāspa",gloss:"the patron who accepts the teaching. Not the Achaemenid Hystaspes, father of Darius, despite a long tradition of confusing the two — the Avestan figure belongs to the eastern Kayanian cycle"},{native:"𐬐𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬌",translit:"kauui",gloss:"in the Gāthās a hostile class of princes; in the Younger Avesta the title of the legendary Kayanian kings, and applied to Vīštāspa himself. A word that changes sides between the two strata"},{native:"𐬐𐬀𐬭𐬀𐬞𐬀𐬥",translit:"karapan",gloss:"one of the priestly classes denounced in the hymns; the sense is disputed, with 'mumbler' the traditional guess and no firm etymology"},{native:"𐬎𐬯𐬌𐬘",translit:"usij",gloss:"a third denounced priestly class, cognate with Vedic uśij — where the word names a perfectly respectable sacrificer. The inversion is part of the evidence for a religious quarrel inside a shared inheritance"},{native:"𐬀𐬴𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬥",translit:"aṣ̌auuan",gloss:"possessing aṣ̌a; the standard designation of the righteous, living or dead, and the word a Zoroastrian name is followed by in liturgical recitation"}],texts:[{title:"Yasna 43–46 (the Uštauuaitī Gāthā)",note:"The hymns with the strongest first-person content, including the complaint at 46.1 about having nowhere to go. Everything biographical rests on a handful of stanzas here."},{title:"Dēnkard, Book 7",note:"Pahlavi, ninth century: the fullest traditional life of Zarathustra, with the miracles, the temptation and the conversion of Vīštāspa. A late systematisation, and the source of most popular accounts."},{title:"Wizīdagīhā ī Zādspram, chapters 12–26",note:"A second Pahlavi life, independent enough of Dēnkard 7 to be worth comparing; the two together are the tradition's own biography, seventeen centuries after the language of the Gāthās."},{title:"W. B. Henning, Zoroaster: Politician or Witch-Doctor? (Oxford, 1951)",note:"The Ratanbai Katrak lectures; a defence of the traditional chronology and a demolition of Herzfeld's reconstruction. Still the sharpest statement of the case for a late date."},{title:"Mary Boyce, A History of Zoroastrianism, vol. 1 (Leiden, 1975), with Gherardo Gnoli, Zoroaster in History (New York, 2000)",note:"The two poles read together: Boyce arguing from language for the second millennium, Gnoli arguing from tradition for the sixth century. The gap between them is the state of the question."}]},{slug:"amesha-spentas",title:"The Wise Lord and the Bounteous Immortals",native:"𐬀𐬨𐬆𐬴𐬀 𐬯𐬞𐬆𐬧𐬙𐬀",translit:"aməṣ̌a spəṇta",summary:"One god named twice over, six entities that are qualities and persons at once, and a heptad that the Gāthās never actually list.",paragraphs:["In Old Avestan the god's name is two words and they behave as two words: mazdā ahura, ahura mazdā, and sometimes separated by a line of verse, each inflected on its own. Ahura is 'lord', the exact cognate of Sanskrit asura; mazdā is a noun of wisdom or attentive memory, so that the name reads as 'the Lord Wisdom' or 'Wisdom the Lord' depending on which is taken as head. Only in Younger Avestan do the two fuse into a single name, Ahurō Mazdā̊, which becomes Middle Persian Ohrmazd. The god is aniconic in the texts and there is no Avestan authority for an image. The winged figure over Achaemenid doorways at Persepolis and Behistun is habitually captioned as Ahuramazdā in museums, but its identification is genuinely unsettled: the candidates include the god, the royal xᵛarənah and a fravashi, and the argument has run since the nineteenth century without resolution.","Around him stand six: Vohu Manah, Good Thought; Aṣ̌a Vahišta, Best Order or Best Truth; Xšaθra Vairiia, Dominion To Be Chosen; Spəṇtā Ārmaiti, Bounteous Right-Mindedness or Devotion; and the pair Hauruuatāt and Amərətāt, Wholeness and Non-Dying. The first three are neuter in Avestan and the last three feminine, and the grammar is not incidental — Ārmaiti is a daughter, and the pairing of the last two is so fixed that they are effectively one entity in two words. They are abstractions with agency: one prays to Good Thought and also thinks with it, and the tradition has never regarded the ambiguity as a problem. Spəṇta itself resists translation. 'Holy' imports the wrong associations; Boyce preferred 'bounteous', Skjærvø 'life-giving', and the underlying sense appears to involve increase or furtherance rather than sanctity.","The heptad, as a fixed list, is a construction of the later texts and not of the Gāthās. The phrase aməṣ̌a spəṇta as a collective term is Younger Avestan; the hymns name these entities individually, in shifting combinations, alongside others that never made the final six, and no Gāthic stanza enumerates them as a body. This is a case where the reader should be plain about what is being read back. The seven — the six plus Ahura Mazdā himself, or in another reckoning plus Spəṇta Mainiiu, his Bounteous Spirit — become a system in the Younger Avesta and a completed architecture in the Pahlavi books. Whether the systematisation draws out what the Gāthās already assume, as Boyce held, or imposes an order the hymns do not have, as the more sceptical readings have it, is not settled and probably cannot be.","The system's great move is to attach each of the seven to a physical creation, so that the material world is divided among them: Vohu Manah takes cattle, Aṣ̌a Vahišta fire, Xšaθra Vairiia metal and the sky, Spəṇtā Ārmaiti the earth, Hauruuatāt the waters, Amərətāt the plants, and Ahura Mazdā humanity. The Bundahišn sets this out fully, and Boyce argued at length that the Yasna ceremony is the doctrine performed — that the priest, handling water, plant, milk, fire and metal in a marked precinct on the earth, is assembling all seven creations and reconstituting the world in miniature. The same seven organise time: the Zoroastrian calendar names its first seven days for them, and their Middle Persian forms still name six of the twelve months of the civil calendar in Iran today, so that anyone in Tehran writing the date in Ordibehesht or Bahman or Esfand is writing Aṣ̌a Vahišta, Vohu Manah and Spəṇtā Ārmaiti without necessarily noticing.","Against Spəṇta Mainiiu stands Aŋra Mainiiu, the Hostile Spirit, and the shape of the opposition matters. In the Gāthās, at Yasna 30 and Yasna 45, the two mainiius choose, and Ahura Mazdā is not one of the pair but the father or the framer of the choice; in the Younger Avesta and consistently in Pahlavi, Ohrmazd faces Ahriman directly, and the structure becomes a symmetrical dualism the older texts do not quite state. A third arrangement, known chiefly from outside, made both of them sons of Zurwān, Time: the myth is reported by the Armenian Eznik of Kołb in the fifth century, and in Greek by Eudemus of Rhodes as preserved by Damascius. R. C. Zaehner, in Zurvan: A Zoroastrian Dilemma (1955), built this into a major heresy dominant under the Sasanians; Boyce and Shaul Shaked argued it was rather a speculative current within the religion than a church. What all three arrangements share, and what distinguishes them from every dualism that settles for permanent war, is that the conflict is bounded: evil is real, it is not the Wise Lord's, and it has an expiry date."],terms:[{native:"𐬀𐬵𐬎𐬭𐬀 𐬨𐬀𐬰𐬛𐬁",translit:"ahura mazdā",gloss:"'Lord Wisdom' or 'the Wise Lord'; two separately inflected words in Old Avestan, a single name in Younger Avestan, and Ohrmazd in Pahlavi"},{native:"𐬬𐬊𐬵𐬎 𐬨𐬀𐬥𐬀𐬵",translit:"vohu manah",gloss:"Good Thought; the entity most frequently addressed in the Gāthās, holder of the cattle among the creations, and Vahman or Bahman in Middle and Modern Persian"},{native:"𐬀𐬴𐬀 𐬬𐬀𐬵𐬌𐬱𐬙𐬀",translit:"aṣ̌a vahišta",gloss:"Best Order; the full name of the entity behind the bare aṣ̌a, holder of fire, and the source of the month-name Ordibehesht in the Iranian calendar"},{native:"𐬑𐬱𐬀𐬚𐬭𐬀 𐬬𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬀",translit:"xšaθra vairiia",gloss:"Dominion To Be Chosen: rule as something elected rather than seized, holder of metal and the stone sky. Šahrewar in Pahlavi, Shahrivar in the modern calendar"},{native:"𐬯𐬞𐬆𐬧𐬙𐬁 𐬁𐬭𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌",translit:"spəṇtā ārmaiti",gloss:"Bounteous Devotion or Right-Mindedness; grammatically feminine, holder of the earth, and addressed as a daughter. Spandarmad, and the month Esfand"},{native:"𐬵𐬀𐬎𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬙𐬁𐬙 𐬀𐬨𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬙𐬁𐬙",translit:"hauruuatāt amərətāt",gloss:"Wholeness and Non-Dying, holders of the waters and the plants; so consistently paired that they function as one, and cognate with the Vedic Sarvatāt and Amṛtatva"}],texts:[{title:"Yasna 30 and Yasna 45",note:"The two stanzaic accounts of the choosing spirits — the Gāthic basis of Zoroastrian dualism, and shorter and more ambiguous than any summary of it."},{title:"Yasna 35–41, the Yasna Haptaŋhāiti",note:"Old Avestan prose worship of fire, waters and the earth. The entities appear here as objects of cult, which is earlier evidence for their personhood than any doctrinal statement."},{title:"Bundahišn (Pahlavi, ninth century), trans. Domenico Agostini and Samuel Thrope (2020)",note:"The systematiser: the seven creations, the assignment of each to an Aməṣ̌a Spəṇta, the bounded time and the assault. Drawing on Avestan material now lost, and not to be mistaken for the Avesta."},{title:"Eznik of Kołb, Refutation of the Sects (Armenian, fifth century)",note:"A hostile Christian witness who preserves the Zurvanite myth of Ohrmazd and Ahriman as twin sons of Time — a version no Zoroastrian source states in that form."},{title:"R. C. Zaehner, Zurvan: A Zoroastrian Dilemma (Oxford, 1955)",note:"The book that made Zurvanism a Sasanian state heresy. Its thesis has been substantially reduced by Boyce and Shaked, and it remains the fullest collection of the evidence."}]},{slug:"the-yasna",title:"The Long Liturgy",native:"𐬯𐬙𐬀𐬊𐬙𐬀 𐬫𐬈𐬯𐬥𐬌𐬌𐬀",translit:"staota yesniia",summary:"Seventy-two chapters, two priests, a pressed plant, a fire and a marked precinct — the ceremony that the whole corpus exists to serve, performed every morning.",paragraphs:["The Yasna is not a book. It is a ceremony of seventy-two chapters, each called a hā or hāiti, and the Gāthās sit inside it rather than the other way round; the tradition matches its seventy-two chapters to the seventy-two threads of the kustī, the cord tied at the waist. At its heart is a block the Avesta itself names, the Staota Yesniia — the 'praises worthy of worship', running from roughly the Ahuna Vairiia to the Ā Airiiəmā Išiiō and enclosing the Gāthās and the Yasna Haptaŋhāiti. This is the point Jean Kellens and Alberto Cantera have pressed hardest: that the Avesta is a liturgical corpus, not a scripture from which liturgies were later extracted, and that reading it as a library of separate books is a nineteenth-century European habit. On this account the Gāthās were preserved because they are recited at a particular point in a ceremony, and they survive with their metre intact because a ceremony does not permit paraphrase.","The ceremony is performed today, in the morning, in the Hāwan gāh between sunrise and noon, and takes something over two hours. Two priests conduct it: the zōt, from Avestan zaotar, who recites, and the rāspī, who tends the fire and answers. The full ancient establishment was larger — Vīsperad 3 names eight offices, among them the hāuuanan at the mortar, the ātrauuaxša who makes the fire blaze, the sraošāuuarəza who supervises, the ābərət who fetches the water — and the reduction from eight to two is one of the few structural changes in the rite that can be traced. The priests work inside a precinct, the pāwī, marked off by furrows cut in the stone floor, into which nothing unconsecrated passes. Only a priest who has undergone the barašnūm purification and the higher initiation may perform it.","The materials are specific and their preparation, the paragnā, is a ceremony in itself. Twigs of haoma, in Indian practice a species of Ephedra obtained from Iran; a twig of pomegranate; milk from a consecrated goat; water drawn from a well; the flat bread called draonah, marked with the fingernail; and the barsom, a bundle of rods held in the left hand, which was once tamarisk or pomegranate twigs and is now, in both Iran and India, a set of metal wires — a change well attested and poorly dated. The haoma is pounded in a metal mortar, and the strokes of the pestle against the rim are themselves part of the sound of the ceremony. What plant stood behind the original haoma has been argued for a century: R. Gordon Wasson proposed the fly agaric for Vedic soma in 1968; Viktor Sarianidi reported traces of ephedra, poppy and cannabis at Togolok-21 and Gonur Depe in Turkmenistan, and subsequent laboratory analysis did not confirm them. Ephedra is what is used now and has been used for as long as the record reaches.","The Yasna is the frame into which longer ceremonies are built. Insert the twenty-odd sections of the Vīsperad — the name is Avestan, vīspe ratauuō, 'all the ratus' — at their appointed places and the result is the Vīsperad service, performed at the six seasonal gāhāmbārs. Insert the Vīdēvdād as well and the result is a night office, performed between midnight and dawn, which is the only context in which that text is liturgically recited. This is the sense in which the Avesta's divisions are performances rather than volumes: the Vīsperad has no independent existence, being a set of interpolations, and the same is largely true of the Vīdēvdād's ritual use.","Who performs it is a question with hard numbers attached. The Indian census of 2011 counted 57,264 Parsis; the Zoroastrian population of Iran is usually estimated at a few tens of thousands, and worldwide totals between one and two hundred thousand are quoted with a candour about their unreliability that does the estimators credit. There are eight Ātaš Bahrāms, fires of the highest grade, in India and one in Yazd; the Irānšāh at Udvada, whose foundation the Qissa-i Sanjan of about 1600 places at Sanjan after the migration from Iran, has been moved repeatedly and is tended continuously. Priestly recruitment is the acute problem: the office is hereditary, the training long, the initiations — nāwar and martab — demanding, and the number of men undertaking them small. The full Yasna has been recorded and studied in performance, notably by Firoze Kotwal and James Boyd, and Mary Boyce's year in Sharifabad in the 1960s remains the best account of the ritual life of an Iranian village. Both are records of a practice, not instructions for one."],terms:[{native:"𐬵𐬁𐬌𐬙𐬌",translit:"hāiti",gloss:"a chapter of the Yasna, seventy-two in all; the Middle Persian hā, and the unit by which the ceremony is counted rather than read"},{native:"𐬰𐬀𐬊𐬚𐬭𐬀",translit:"zaoθra",gloss:"libation; the liquid offering poured to the waters and the fire, from the same root as zaotar — the priest is named from what he pours"},{native:"𐬛𐬭𐬀𐬊𐬥𐬀𐬵",translit:"draonah",gloss:"the consecrated portion; the flat bread marked and shared in the ceremony, still called drōn"},{native:"𐬁𐬙𐬭𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬑𐬱𐬀",translit:"ātrauuaxša",gloss:"'he who makes the fire grow'; one of the eight priestly offices of the full ancient ceremony, absorbed today into the duties of the rāspī"},{native:"𐬯𐬭𐬀𐬊𐬱𐬁𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬰𐬀",translit:"sraošāuuarəza",gloss:"the supervising priest, named from Sraoša, Hearkening; the office that watched the correctness of the performance, which tells you what the tradition thought was at risk"},{native:"𐬬𐬍𐬯𐬞𐬈 𐬭𐬀𐬙𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬋",translit:"vīspe ratauuō",gloss:"'all the ratus', the lords of the ritual divisions; the phrase from which the Vīsperad takes its name, and a text that exists only as insertions into another"}],texts:[{title:"Yasna 1–72",note:"The ceremony entire, with the Gāthās and the Yasna Haptaŋhāiti embedded in it. Reading it as a sequence rather than mining it for doctrine is the single most useful discipline in the subject."},{title:"Vīsperad",note:"Some two dozen short sections, having no independent life: they are interpolations that turn the Yasna into the seasonal service. The clearest demonstration that the Avesta's 'books' are performances."},{title:"Firoze M. Kotwal and James W. Boyd, A Persian Offering: The Yasna, a Zoroastrian High Liturgy (Paris, 1991)",note:"A high priest and a scholar of religion documenting the ceremony action by action. Descriptive throughout, and the standard reference for what is actually done."},{title:"Mary Boyce, A Persian Stronghold of Zoroastrianism (Oxford, 1977)",note:"Fieldwork from a year in Sharifabad near Yazd in the 1960s; the ritual year of an Iranian Zoroastrian village recorded before it changed."},{title:"Jean Kellens, 'Considérations sur l'histoire de l'Avesta', Journal Asiatique 286 (1998)",note:"The article that put the liturgical understanding of the corpus at the centre of the field, and against which subsequent work on transmission is written."}]},{slug:"the-two-prayers",title:"Twenty-One Words and Twelve",native:"𐬀𐬵𐬎𐬥𐬀 𐬬𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬀",translit:"ahuna vairiia",summary:"The two short formulae every Zoroastrian knows, one of which was recited before the world was made, and neither of which anyone can translate with confidence.",paragraphs:["The Ahuna Vairiia stands at Yasna 27.13, and the tradition counts its words: twenty-one, matched to the twenty-one nasks of the Sasanian Avesta, so that the prayer contains the scripture in compressed form. It is Old Avestan, in the metre that gives the first Gāthā its name, and it is recited constantly — at the start of ceremonies, at the tying of the kustī, at undertakings and departures. Yasna 19 is an Avestan commentary on it, one of a set of three (Yasna 19, 20 and 21) that gloss the great prayers in the old language, and it reports that Ahura Mazdā recited this formula before the creation. The Bundahišn draws the consequence: at its utterance Aŋra Mainiiu was confounded and fell back into the darkness for three thousand years, which is how the world got the time it needed. A prayer, in this account, is not a request made within the created order. It is the thing said in order that there should be one.","What it means is another matter. The opening line turns on two words, ahu and ratu, and everything follows from how they are construed. Stanley Insler read the first as a lord who must be chosen in accordance with truth, and the second as the judgement that follows on the same principle; Helmut Humbach took ahu and ratu as the two authorities of the Zoroastrian order, the temporal and the spiritual; Jean Kellens has cautioned that the stanza's syntax may not be recoverable at all, the sentence having been transmitted as a sound long after it stopped being parsed. The second half is less contested only in the sense that it is less discussed: it speaks of the gift of Good Thought for deeds done in this existence for Mazdā, and of dominion belonging to the Lord whom they establish as shepherd for the poor. The last phrase — pastor to the dependent and the destitute — is the one clear ethical statement in the prayer, and it is the reason the formula has been read as a charter rather than a spell.","The Aṣ̌əm Vohū at Yasna 27.14 is shorter still: twelve words, glossed by Yasna 20. It says that aṣ̌a is the best good, that it is weal, and that weal belongs to him whose aṣ̌a is for the sake of best aṣ̌a — a sentence that folds back on itself twice, once on aṣ̌a and once on the word uštā, and that turns on refusing to make truth instrumental. Doing right for the sake of right, rather than for the weal that follows, is what the twelve words appear to insist on, and they insist on it by a syntactic figure rather than an argument. It is the prayer a Zoroastrian says most often, taught to children, counted in sets, recited before eating and before sleep. Its brevity is not incidental to its survival.","Two more complete the set of great formulae. The Yeŋ́hē Hātąm, a short doxology derived from a stanza of the Gāthās at Yasna 51.22, names those men and women whom Ahura Mazdā knows to be better in worship according to aṣ̌a, and it is the formula by which the yazatas are addressed collectively. The Ā Airiiəmā Išiiō, at Yasna 54.1, closes the Staota Yesniia; it calls on Airiiaman, the divinity of the bond and of healing, and the tradition assigns it a role at the end of things, when Airiiaman is to come for the final healing at the frašō.kərəti. So the liturgy's core is bracketed by two prayers, one recited before creation and one associated with its repair, which is a piece of architecture rather than an accident.","How these formulae were used is visible in an unexpected place. The oldest surviving copy of any Avestan text is a fragment of the Aṣ̌əm Vohū recovered from Dunhuang, on the eastern edge of the Tarim basin, written in Sogdian script and generally dated to the ninth century. It is not in the Avestan alphabet; its phonetic form suggests transmission through a Middle Persian intermediary; and it was carried some two thousand miles from Fārs by somebody who wanted it. The Pahlavi books elaborate an arithmetic of recitation, assigning merit to fixed numbers of repetitions, and the counting of Aṣ̌əm Vohūs remains ordinary practice. It is worth stating what the evidence actually shows, which is that these two formulae travelled further, survived better and were used more than any other part of the corpus — and that they are the two shortest texts in it, and among the least intelligible."],terms:[{native:"𐬫𐬀𐬚𐬁 𐬀𐬵𐬏 𐬬𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬋",translit:"yaθā ahū vairiiō",gloss:"the opening words of the Ahuna Vairiia, by which the prayer is commonly named; every English rendering of them is a decision about two disputed nouns"},{native:"𐬀𐬵𐬎",translit:"ahu",gloss:"existence, or lord; the first of the two words on which the prayer turns, and the ambiguity is real rather than a translator's difficulty"},{native:"𐬭𐬀𐬙𐬎",translit:"ratu",gloss:"model, judge, the proper time or the proper measure of a thing; the second disputed word, and the term from which the Vīsperad's 'all the ratus' is formed"},{native:"𐬀𐬴𐬆𐬨 𐬬𐬊𐬵𐬏",translit:"aṣ̌əm vohū",gloss:"'aṣ̌a is good'; twelve words, glossed by Yasna 20, and the most frequently recited sentence in the religion"},{native:"𐬫𐬈𐬣𐬵𐬉 𐬵𐬁𐬙𐬄𐬨",translit:"yeŋ́hē hātąm",gloss:"'of whom among those that are'; the short doxology drawn from Yasna 51.22 and used to address the yazatas as a body, women named alongside men"},{native:"𐬁 𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬆𐬨𐬁 𐬌𐬱𐬌𐬌𐬋",translit:"ā airiiəmā išiiō",gloss:"the fourth great prayer, at Yasna 54.1, invoking Airiiaman of the bond and of healing; it closes the Staota Yesniia and is assigned a part in the final restoration"}],texts:[{title:"Yasna 27.13–14",note:"The Ahuna Vairiia and the Aṣ̌əm Vohū in their liturgical position, at the threshold of the ceremony rather than in the Gāthās proper, though both are Old Avestan."},{title:"Yasna 19, 20 and 21",note:"Avestan commentaries on the three prayers — exegesis composed in the sacred language itself, and the source of the claim that the Ahuna Vairiia was recited before creation."},{title:"Yasna 54.1, the Ā Airiiəmā Išiiō",note:"The closing prayer of the Staota Yesniia; short, and carrying the tradition's association between Airiiaman, healing and the end of the world."},{title:"The Sogdian Ashem Vohu fragment, Dunhuang (British Library)",note:"Ninth century, in Sogdian script; the earliest surviving witness to any Avestan text, and evidence for how far and in what form the short prayers travelled."},{title:"Helmut Humbach, The Gāthās of Zarathushtra and the Other Old Avestan Texts, vol. 2 (Heidelberg, 1991)",note:"The commentary volume sets out the competing constructions of the Ahuna Vairiia side by side. The place to see exactly how much of the prayer is undecided."}]},{slug:"the-yashts",title:"The Hymns to the Yazatas",native:"𐬨𐬌𐬚𐬭𐬀",translit:"miθra",summary:"Twenty-one hymns to divinities older than the religion that kept them: the god of the contract with ten thousand eyes, the star that fights drought in the shape of a white horse, and a glory that leaves a king in three flights.",paragraphs:["The Yašts are twenty-one hymns of wildly unequal length and quality, addressed to individual yazatas — 'beings worthy of worship' — and they are Younger Avestan throughout. Several are little more than liturgical stubs; a handful are long narrative poems and among the finest surviving Iranian literature. They are transmitted as prose, but they are not prose: the great Yašts are composed in an eight-syllable line, and restoring the metre is a philological operation that has run from Friedrich Carl Andreas and Geldner through Hanns-Peter Schmidt and Almut Hintze without producing agreement about how far the restoration should go. What the Yašts principally preserve is a layer of religion older than Zarathustra. Their divinities have Vedic counterparts — Miθra beside Mitra, Vərəθraγna beside Vṛtrahan, Vaiiu beside Vāyu, Apąm Napāt beside Apāṃ Napāt, Tištriia beside Tiṣya — and their formulae are Indo-Iranian inheritance. A tradition that rejected the daēuuas kept these, and how that selection was made is one of the field's genuinely open questions.","Yašt 10, the Mihr Yašt, runs to thirty-five sections and is the longest sustained composition in the corpus after the Frawardīn Yašt. Its subject is Miθra of the wide pastures, and the first thing to understand is that miθra means contract: the god is the personified bond between parties, and his hostility is directed at the miθrō.druj, the man who breaks an agreement. He has a thousand ears and ten thousand eyes, he never sleeps, he watches over the whole Aryan expanse from a chariot, and he is emphatically not the sun — he rises before it, ahead of the immortal swift-horsed sun, over the peak of Harā. Ilya Gershevitch's edition of 1959 remains the standard treatment. Whether this figure has anything substantial to do with the Roman Mithras is a separate and much-fought question: Franz Cumont assumed direct continuity, and since the 1970s John Hinnells, Richard Gordon and Roger Beck have largely dismantled that assumption, leaving the name, and probably not much else, as the demonstrable connection.","Yašt 5, the Ābān Yašt, is addressed to Arəduuī Sūrā Anāhitā — 'the Moist, the Strong, the Undefiled' — the river-goddess who pours from the peak of Hukairiia into the sea Vourukaša. It is unlike anything else in the Avesta in one respect: it describes her physically, in detail, as a tall strong maiden with a golden crown of eight rays set with a hundred stars, golden earrings and a square golden necklace, wearing a mantle of thirty beaver skins. This concreteness has been taken as evidence of accretion from a Mesopotamian goddess of the Ištar type, and the argument has external support: Artaxerxes II is the first Achaemenid king whose inscriptions name gods beside Ahuramazdā, and the gods he names are Anāhitā and Miθra, while Berossus reports that he set up statues of Anāhitā in several cities. An aniconic tradition acquiring an image and a description at a datable moment is not a common thing to be able to watch.","Two hymns show the older mythology at work. In Yašt 8, Tištriia — the star Sirius, whose rising brings the rains — descends to the sea Vourukaša in the form of a white horse with golden ears, and there meets the demon Apaoša in the form of a black horse; Tištriia is beaten and driven back, and he complains that men have not worshipped him with a sacrifice in which his name is spoken, so that he has no strength. Ahura Mazdā then offers him that sacrifice, and Tištriia returns and wins, and the waters flow. It is a candid statement of what the tradition thinks worship is for. In Yašt 14, Vərəθraγna, Smiting-of-Resistance, appears to Zarathustra in ten successive forms: a wind, a bull with golden horns, a white horse, a rutting camel, a boar, a youth of fifteen, a bird of prey, a ram, a wild goat and an armed man. The Vedic cognate is an epithet of Indra; in the Avesta Indra is a demon and this is an independent god.","Yašt 19, the Zamyād Yašt, is the hymn of the xᵛarənah, the fortune or glory that attaches to legitimate rule, and it contains the passage on which the whole later Iranian idea of kingship rests. The glory belongs to Yima while he reigns rightly; when he takes to falsehood it leaves him in three flights, going first to Miθra, then to Θraētaona — who bound Aži Dahāka — and then to Kərəsāspa, and each departure costs Yima a portion of his sovereignty. The hymn then runs the Kayanian line, kauui by kauui, and ends with the glory resting on the future Saošiiaṇt. Almut Hintze's edition of 1994 is the standard text. It is worth following where this material goes: through the Pahlavi books and the lost Xwadāy-nāmag it becomes the dynastic backbone of Ferdowsi's Shāhnāma, so that a Younger Avestan hymn to a divine glory is the remote ancestor of the Persian national epic — and of the farr that Iranian political language still invokes."],terms:[{native:"𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬛𐬎𐬎𐬍 𐬯𐬏𐬭𐬁 𐬀𐬥𐬁𐬵𐬌𐬙𐬁",translit:"arəduuī sūrā anāhitā",gloss:"'the Moist, the Strong, the Undefiled'; the river-goddess of Yašt 5, and the only figure in the Avesta given a sustained physical description"},{native:"𐬙𐬌𐬱𐬙𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬀",translit:"tištriia",gloss:"Sirius; the star whose heliacal rising brings the rains, who fights the drought-demon Apaoša as a white horse against a black, and loses until he is sacrificed to"},{native:"𐬬𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬚𐬭𐬀𐬖𐬥𐬀",translit:"vərəθraγna",gloss:"'smiting of resistance'; the yazata of victory, who appears in ten animal and human forms in Yašt 14. Bahrām in Middle and New Persian, and hence a planet and a great many kings"},{native:"𐬨𐬌𐬚𐬭𐬋𐬛𐬭𐬎𐬘",translit:"miθrō.druj",gloss:"the contract-breaker: one who lies against the bond. The compound joins the two great terms of the tradition, and identifies the specific offence Miθra exists to punish"},{native:"𐬀𐬱𐬌",translit:"aši",gloss:"Reward or Recompense, a feminine yazata with her own hymn (Yašt 17); the personification of what accrues to right action, and sister to Sraoša in the later reckoning"},{native:"𐬀𐬞𐬄𐬨 𐬥𐬀𐬞𐬁𐬙",translit:"apąm napāt",gloss:"'Descendant of the Waters', a divinity with an exact Vedic counterpart and no Zoroastrian reworking at all; one of the clearest survivals of the shared Indo-Iranian pantheon"}],texts:[{title:"Yašt 10, the Mihr Yašt, ed. Ilya Gershevitch, The Avestan Hymn to Mithra (Cambridge, 1959)",note:"Text, translation and a commentary that is still the standard entry to Younger Avestan poetics. Gershevitch's readings are contested; the edition is not replaceable."},{title:"Yašt 5, the Ābān Yašt",note:"The hymn to Arəduuī Sūrā Anāhitā, with the physical description at stanzas 126–129. Read against the inscriptions of Artaxerxes II, which name her alongside Ahuramazdā and Miθra."},{title:"Yašt 8, the Tištar Yašt",note:"The duel of the white and black horses at the sea Vourukaša, and the frankest statement in the corpus of what the tradition believes sacrifice supplies."},{title:"Yašt 13, the Frawardīn Yašt",note:"The longest of the Yašts, running to more than 150 stanzas, and a roll-call of the frauuaṣ̌is of the righteous. The single richest source for Avestan personal names and for the legendary history."},{title:"Yašt 19, the Zamyād Yašt, ed. Almut Hintze, Der Zamyād-Yašt (Wiesbaden, 1994)",note:"The hymn of the xᵛarənah, the three flights from Yima and the Kayanian line; the root of the Iranian doctrine of royal glory and, at long remove, of the Shāhnāma's dynastic frame."}]},{slug:"the-videvdad",title:"The Law Against the Demons",native:"𐬬𐬍𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬎𐬎𐬋𐬛𐬁𐬙𐬀",translit:"vīdaēuuō.dāta",summary:"Twenty-two chapters of geography, myth and pollution law: the only nask of the Sasanian Avesta to survive entire, and the one the tradition's own reformers would rather have lost.",paragraphs:["The name means 'the law given against the daēuuas' — vī-daēuua-dāta — and the familiar form Vendidad is a Middle Persian contraction of it. Its importance is accidental and enormous: of the twenty-one nasks that Dēnkard 8 catalogues as constituting the Sasanian Avesta, this is the only one that has come down complete. Everything else is fragments, liturgical extracts and Pahlavi summaries. It has twenty-two chapters, called fargards, and it is a composite: myth in the first two, cosmological anecdote in the third, and then long stretches of purity law framed as dialogue, with Zarathustra asking and Ahura Mazdā answering. Its Avestan is late and often bad — a language reconstructed by writers for whom it was no longer native, with case endings misapplied and syntax that a Gāthic poet would not recognise. Geldner noticed this and every editor since has confirmed it. The text is therefore old in material and late in composition, and separating the two is much of the work.","Fargard 1 lists sixteen lands created in turn by Ahura Mazdā, each answered by a counter-creation of Aŋra Mainiiu. The first is Airiianəm Vaējō, the Aryan Expanse, and the counter-creation given to it is the serpent and a winter of ten months. After it come lands that can be identified: Suγδa, Sogdiana, afflicted with the fly that kills cattle; Mouru, Margiana; Bāxδī, Bactria with its high banners; Nisāya; Harōiuua, Areia; Vaēkərəta; Uruua; Haraxᵛaitī, Arachosia; Haētumaṇt, the Helmand; Raγa; Varəna; Hapta Həṇdu, the Seven Rivers. Not one is in Persia or Media. Whatever else the list is, it is a map of the eastern Iranian world made by people who lived in it, and it is the strongest single piece of evidence for the eastern setting of the whole Avestan tradition.","Fargard 2 is the Yima story, and it deserves to be read for what it says rather than for what it resembles. Ahura Mazdā warns Yima that a terrible winter is coming, snow to the depth of many fingers, and instructs him to build a vara — an enclosure, laid out as a square of a certain measure with streets and dwellings, into which he is to bring the seeds of the best men and women, the best cattle, the best plants, the best fires, two of every kind, and no deformity, no illness, no malice. It is lit from within: the text says there are lights uncreated and lights created there, and that a year there seems as a day. The parallel to Mesopotamian flood narratives is real and is limited by the difference at the centre of it — this is a winter, not a flood, and the vara is not a boat. The Pahlavi tradition later makes the vara a place where the survivors wait for the end of the world.","The purity law is the bulk of the text and its logic is consistent. Death is the great victory of the hostile spirit, and a corpse is where that victory is concentrated: the Nasu, the corpse-demon, is imagined arriving from the north in the form of a fly, and everything the law prescribes is aimed at containing her. Hence the exposure of the dead on a daxma rather than burial or cremation, which would defile earth or fire; hence the sagdīd, the gaze of a dog — preferably one with a mark above each eye, the 'four-eyed' dog — which drives the Nasu off; hence the barəšnūm ī nō šwa, the nine-nights purification set out in Fargard 9, still performed and still required of priests before the higher initiation. The dog's standing in this text is extraordinary and has no close parallel: Fargard 13 legislates for dogs at length, and the killing of the water-dog, the otter, carries the most extravagant penalties in the corpus. Those penalties — thousands of strokes with two named implements — are so far beyond the physically possible that they were understood, in the Pahlavi commentary, as fines at a fixed rate of conversion.","Two present-tense facts and one honest difficulty. The daxmas above Yazd have stood empty since the 1970s, when Iranian Zoroastrians moved to burial in graves lined with concrete, which is a solution to the same problem by other means; the towers at Doongerwadi in Mumbai remain in use, but the vultures that did the work collapsed across South Asia in the 1990s after the veterinary drug diclofenac entered the food chain, and solar concentrators were installed to compensate. The difficulty is internal. The Vīdēvdād is the text that reformist Parsis of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries most wished to demote, and the arguments about its authority have never entirely ended; it is also still recited in the night ceremony that bears its name. And it contains the sharpest evidence of the great inversion — Iṇdra, Sauruua and Nāŋhaiθiia named as daēuuas, three gods who in India are Indra, Śarva and the Nāsatyas. What that inversion means, and whether it records a reform, a schism or a slow drift, is not settled and the tidy story of a prophet demoting the old gods is more confident than the evidence."],terms:[{native:"𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬎",translit:"nasu",gloss:"the corpse-demon, grammatically feminine, imagined as a fly coming from the north; the entity the whole apparatus of purity law is designed to contain"},{native:"𐬛𐬀𐬑𐬨𐬀",translit:"daxma",gloss:"the structure for exposing the dead, so that neither earth nor fire nor water is defiled; the Towers of Silence of English usage, and the word is Avestan"},{native:"𐬠𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬱𐬥𐬏𐬨",translit:"barəšnūm",gloss:"the nine-nights purification of Fargard 9, performed in a marked enclosure with consecrated bull's urine, sand and water; still required of a priest before the higher initiation"},{native:"𐬯𐬞𐬁",translit:"spā",gloss:"dog; an animal of the good creation with its own chapter of law, whose gaze drives off the Nasu and whose killing is among the gravest offences the text knows"},{native:"𐬬𐬀𐬭𐬀",translit:"vara",gloss:"the enclosure Yima builds against the great winter, square, lit from within, holding two of every good kind; not an ark, and the difference is the point"},{native:"𐬫𐬁𐬙𐬎 𐬞𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬐𐬁",translit:"yātu pairikā",gloss:"sorcerer and witch, a fixed pair in the texts; pairikā survives into Persian as parī and into English, at several removes, as peri"}],texts:[{title:"Vīdēvdād, Fargard 1",note:"The sixteen lands and their counter-creations. The geography is entirely eastern, and it is the single strongest argument for where the Avestan tradition was formed."},{title:"Vīdēvdād, Fargard 2",note:"Yima, the coming winter and the vara. Read for what it says rather than for the flood story it is habitually compared with."},{title:"Vīdēvdād, Fargard 9",note:"The barəšnūm ī nō šwa set out step by step — the fullest description of a purification rite anywhere in the Avesta, and a rite still performed."},{title:"Hērbedestān and Nērangestān, ed. F. M. Kotwal and P. G. Kreyenbroek, 4 vols (Paris, 1992–2009)",note:"The Avestan texts on priestly training and ritual law, with the Pahlavi commentary; the technical literature that stands behind the Vīdēvdād's provisions."},{title:"James Darmesteter, The Zend-Avesta, Part I: The Vendîdâd, Sacred Books of the East IV (Oxford, 2nd ed. 1895)",note:"The accessible English version, and dated in exactly the ways a translation of 1895 will be. Useful for the shape of the text, not for its philology."}]},{slug:"the-soul-and-the-end",title:"The Bridge and the Making Wonderful",native:"𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬥𐬁",translit:"daēnā",summary:"Three nights beside the body, a girl of fifteen who is the sum of what you did, a bridge that widens or narrows, and a world eventually repaired for everybody — including the wicked.",paragraphs:["The tradition does not have a soul; it has five, or five aspects, and the list is given in the liturgy itself. Yasna 26 and Yašt 13 name ahu, the vital force; daēnā, which is variously insight, conscience, vision and the religion itself; baoδah, perception; uruuan, the soul that acts and is judged; and frauuaṣ̌i, the pre-existent spirit that belongs to each person and does not descend into the world with them. The frauuaṣ̌is are the strangest of the five and the most vividly imagined. Yašt 13 opens with Ahura Mazdā telling Zarathustra that it was by their strength that he held apart the sky and the earth; they appear like well-winged birds, they fight for their own descendants in battle, and they come back. At the last ten days of the year, at Frawardīgān — Muktad, in Parsi usage — they return to the houses, and the houses are cleaned and flowers and water set out for them, and this is done now, every year, in Mumbai and Yazd and wherever else there are Zoroastrians.","What happens to the uruuan is described in a fragment, Hadōxt Nask 2, which is one of the finest things in the corpus. The soul of the righteous man sits for three nights at the head of the corpse, and in those three nights it experiences as much joy as all the living world contains. At the dawn of the fourth day a wind comes from the south, fragrant, more fragrant than any other wind, and in it his own daēnā comes to meet him in the form of a maiden of fifteen, beautiful, bright, strong, high-breasted, of the shape most beautiful. He asks who she is, and she answers that she is his own daēnā: that she was lovely and he has made her lovelier, desirable and he has made her more desirable, by his good thought, his good word, his good deed. For the wicked the same structure runs in reverse — a foul wind from the north, and a hag. Then the soul takes three steps, into Good Thought, Good Word and Good Deed, and a fourth into the Lights Without Beginning.","The bridge is Avestan and most of what people know about it is not. The Činuuatō Pərətu, the Crossing of the Separator, appears in the Gāthās themselves — at Yasna 46.10 and 11, and at 51.13, where the soul of the false one misses the way at the bridge — and in the Younger Avesta at Vīdēvdād 19. Three judges attend it in the later tradition: Miθra, Sraoša and Rašnu, who holds the scales. But the detail everyone repeats, that the bridge is broad for the righteous and narrows to a razor's edge for the wicked, is Pahlavi and not Avestan; it belongs to the Ardā Wirāz Nāmag and the Dādestān ī Dēnīg. So does hammistagān, the 'place of the mixed', where those whose good and evil deeds balance exactly wait without torment and without joy until the end. It resembles purgatory and is not one: nothing is purged there, and nobody prays anyone out.","The end is called frašō.kərəti in Avestan and frašgird in Pahlavi, the making-wonderful, and its agent is the Saošiiaṇt, the Benefactor. Yašt 13 and Yašt 19 name three, born in sequence at intervals of a thousand years from the seed of Zarathustra preserved in the lake Kąsaoiia, the last of them Astuuat̰.ərəta, 'he who embodies aṣ̌a'. The Pahlavi books complete the picture: the dead rise in their bodies, the metals of the mountains melt and flow over the earth in a flood, and all mankind passes through it — and to the righteous it feels like walking through warm milk, and to the wicked it is what molten metal is. The Gāthās hint at this at Yasna 51.9, where a judgement by glowing metal is invoked. And then the ending that has no close parallel: in the Bundahišn the ordeal purifies rather than destroys, hell is emptied and cleansed, Ahriman is made powerless, and the whole creation is restored. Salvation, in the end, is universal.","Two cautions have to be attached to all of this, and they are the reason the current ends here rather than with the restoration. First, the proportions are misleading: the eschatology is sketched in the Avesta and elaborated in the Pahlavi books of the ninth century, written under Islamic rule by priests systematising an inheritance they knew was under pressure — the Bundahišn, the Dēnkard, the Ardā Wirāz Nāmag, the Mēnōg ī Xrad. Much of what is called Zoroastrian eschatology is theirs. Second, the question everyone eventually asks — whether this material shaped Jewish, Christian and Islamic ideas of resurrection, judgement and a final restoration — is genuinely open and is made unanswerable by exactly the problem this hall began with. Mary Boyce argued for substantial and early Iranian influence during the Achaemenid period; Shaul Shaked argued more cautiously for contact and mutual availability of ideas; the sceptical position notes that the fullest Zoroastrian statements postdate the Jewish and Christian ones by centuries and that the Avestan evidence is thin where it would need to be thick. Nobody has won this argument. Anyone who tells you it is settled is telling you which side they are on."],terms:[{native:"𐬠𐬀𐬊𐬜𐬀𐬵",translit:"baoδah",gloss:"perception or consciousness; one of the five components of the person named in the liturgy, alongside ahu, daēnā, uruuan and frauuaṣ̌i"},{native:"𐬵𐬎𐬨𐬀𐬙𐬀 𐬵𐬏𐬑𐬙𐬀 𐬵𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬭𐬱𐬙𐬀",translit:"humata hūxta huuaršta",gloss:"good thought, good word, good deed — the tradition's own summary of itself, and in Hadōxt Nask the three stations the righteous soul steps through before the endless lights"},{native:"𐬔𐬀𐬭𐬋𐬛𐬆𐬨𐬁𐬥𐬀",translit:"garō.dəmāna",gloss:"the House of Song, the highest heaven; Middle Persian garōdmān. That the best place is named for singing rather than for light or rest is worth pausing over"},{native:"𐬛𐬭𐬎𐬘𐬋𐬛𐬆𐬨𐬁𐬥𐬀",translit:"drujō.dəmāna",gloss:"the House of the Lie; the Gāthic name for the destination of the wicked, built from druj rather than from any word for punishment"},{native:"𐬭𐬀𐬱𐬥𐬎",translit:"rašnu",gloss:"the yazata of the balance, who weighs at the bridge; the tradition's judgement is an act of measurement rather than of verdict"},{native:"𐬯𐬀𐬊𐬱𐬌𐬌𐬀𐬧𐬙",translit:"saošiiaṇt",gloss:"the Benefactor who brings the world's repair; used in the Gāthās in the plural and of the living, and narrowed by the later texts to three future figures born from Zarathustra's preserved seed"}],texts:[{title:"Hadōxt Nask, Fragment 2",note:"The three nights at the head of the corpse, the wind at the fourth dawn, and the daēnā who comes as a maiden of fifteen. The surviving remnant of a lost nask, and the most vivid Avestan text on the soul."},{title:"Yasna 46.10–11 and 51.13",note:"The Činuuatō Pərətu in the Gāthās themselves — brief, allusive, and the whole Old Avestan basis for a doctrine the later tradition builds out enormously."},{title:"Yašt 13, the Frawardīn Yašt",note:"The frauuaṣ̌is: pre-existent, warlike, returning at the year's end, and credited with holding sky and earth apart. Also the fullest Avestan list of names in existence."},{title:"Ardā Wirāz Nāmag (Pahlavi)",note:"A priest drugged and sent for seven days to view heaven and hell, and to report. A visionary journey of the ninth century or thereabouts; the source of the razor-edged bridge and of most later imagery."},{title:"Bundahišn, chapters on the frašgird (Pahlavi, ninth century), trans. Agostini and Thrope (2020)",note:"The resurrection, the flood of molten metal, the cleansing of hell and the universal restoration. The systematisation, stated as such — the Avesta nowhere sets it out at this length."}]}]};export{e as default};
